7. Workflow Instantiation

Every execution SHALL create a unique Workflow Instance.

Each instance SHALL define:

Property	Description
Instance ID	Globally unique identifier
Workflow Definition	Referenced definition
Workflow Version	Executed version
Execution Context	Applicable environment
Start Time	Execution start
Initiating Authority	Responsible initiator
Initial State	Starting execution state

Instances SHALL remain immutable historical records.

8. Execution Context

Every execution SHALL resolve:

Applicable policies
Required inputs
Participants
Resources
Environment
Permissions
Dependencies
Time constraints

Execution SHALL NOT begin until the required context is valid.

9. Activity Execution

Each activity SHALL proceed through:

Ready
   │
Assigned
   │
Started
   │
Executing
   │
Completed

Or

Executing
      │
      ▼
Failed
      │
      ▼
Recovered

Activity history SHALL remain immutable.

10. State Management

Workflow state SHALL be explicitly managed.

Atlas defines the following canonical execution states.

State	Description
Initialized	Instance created
Ready	Awaiting execution
Running	Active execution
Waiting	Awaiting event or dependency
Suspended	Temporarily paused
Recovering	Recovery in progress
Completed	Successfully finished
Failed	Execution terminated unsuccessfully
Cancelled	Intentionally terminated

State transitions SHALL conform to AWS-006 — Workflow State Model.

11. Event Generation

Execution SHALL generate Canonical Events including:

Workflow Started
Activity Started
Activity Completed
Activity Failed
Participant Assigned
Approval Granted
Workflow Suspended
Workflow Resumed
Workflow Completed

Events SHALL conform to ADS-006 — Canonical Event (CEV).

12. Failure Handling

Failures SHALL define:

Failure identifier
Failed activity
Failure cause
Severity
Recovery strategy
Responsible authority
Generated events

Failure SHALL NOT invalidate completed historical activities.

13. Recovery

Recovery MAY include:

Retry activity
Resume workflow
Restart activity
Escalate
Invoke compensating workflow
Cancel execution

Recovery SHALL preserve execution history.

14. Completion

A workflow SHALL complete when:

All mandatory activities succeed.
Completion conditions are satisfied.
Required outputs are produced.
Applicable policies are satisfied.

Completion SHALL generate a Completion Event.

15. Execution Trace

Every Workflow Instance SHALL preserve:

Activity history
State transitions
Decisions
Events
Participants
Timing
Inputs
Outputs

The execution trace SHALL be immutable.

16. Execution Governance

Every execution SHALL define:

Execution owner
Responsible authority
Applicable governance domain
Active policies
Audit requirements
Compliance obligations

Governance SHALL remain attached throughout execution.

17. Quality Requirements

Every execution SHALL satisfy:

Correctness
Determinism
Explainability
Traceability
Recoverability
Auditability
Reproducibility
Governance compliance

Quality SHALL be continuously monitored.

18. Conformance

A Workflow Execution implementation conforms if it:

Creates canonical Workflow Instances.
Preserves immutable definitions.
Supports deterministic execution.
Generates canonical events.
Maintains execution history.
Supports governed recovery.
Preserves complete traceability.
19. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

AI-assisted execution optimization.
Autonomous workflow execution.
Distributed execution coordination.
Predictive failure recovery.
Digital Twin synchronization.
Adaptive execution strategies.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical execution semantics.

